# `module::Field::model_name`

[Back to `module::Field`](../Field.md)

The name of the model the field holds, as text.

## Signature

```cpp
std::string module::Field::model_name() const;
```

## What It Does

Returns one of `"BlackScholes"`, `"Sabr"`, `"Heston"`, `"Bates"`, `"RoughHeston"`, `"RoughBergomi"`. It is the same text as [`Model::name`](../../model/Model/name.md); use it for labels and logs.

## Returns

The model name.

## Practical Examples

### Example 1

The name of a Black-Scholes and a Heston field.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace fin = pluss::subsp::math::finance;
namespace mod = fin::module;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  mod::Field field{.instrument = fin::instrument::make(fin::pricing::Call, 100.0, 1.0),
                   .market = fin::market::make(100.0, 0.05, 0.02),
                   .model = fin::model::make(fin::model::Kind::BlackScholes, {0.2}),
                   .controls = {.engine = mod::Engine::Analytic, .risk = mod::Risk::All}};
  mod::Field heston{.instrument = fin::instrument::make(fin::pricing::Call, 100.0, 1.0),
                   .market = fin::market::make(100.0, 0.02, 0.0),
                   .model = fin::model::make(fin::model::Kind::Heston, {1.5, 0.04, 0.3, -0.7, 0.04}),
                   .controls = {.engine = mod::Engine::Cos, .risk = mod::Risk::PriceOnly}};

  out.writeln(field.model_name(), heston.model_name());
}
```

### Example 2

It follows the model: replace the model and the name changes.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace fin = pluss::subsp::math::finance;
namespace mod = fin::module;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  mod::Field field{.instrument = fin::instrument::make(fin::pricing::Call, 100.0, 1.0),
                   .market = fin::market::make(100.0, 0.02, 0.0),
                   .model = fin::model::make(fin::model::Kind::Heston, {1.5, 0.04, 0.3, -0.7, 0.04}),
                   .controls = {.engine = mod::Engine::Cos, .risk = mod::Risk::PriceOnly}};

  out.writeln("before:", field.model_name());
  field.model = fin::model::make(fin::model::Kind::Sabr, {0.2, 1.0, 0.4, -0.3});
  out.writeln("after :", field.model_name());
}
```

### Example 3

All six names, from models built by kind.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>
#include <pluss/tils/kit/output.h>

#include <vector>

using namespace pluss::tils;

namespace fin = pluss::subsp::math::finance;
namespace mod = fin::module;
using pluss::Series;

int main() {
  OutputEngine out;
  out.print_defaults(PrintOptions{}.fixed(4));
  struct Case { fin::model::Kind kind; std::vector<double> params; };
  const std::vector<Case> cases = {{fin::model::Kind::BlackScholes, {0.2}}, {fin::model::Kind::Sabr, {0.2, 1.0, 0.4, -0.3}}, {fin::model::Kind::Heston, {1.5, 0.04, 0.3, -0.7, 0.04}},
                                   {fin::model::Kind::Bates, {1.5, 0.04, 0.3, -0.7, 0.04, 0.5, -0.1, 0.2}}, {fin::model::Kind::RoughHeston, {1.5, 0.04, 0.3, -0.3, -0.7, 0.04}}, {fin::model::Kind::RoughBergomi, {0.04, 1.5, -0.1, -0.7}}};
  for (const Case &c : cases) {
    mod::Field field;
    field.model = fin::model::make(c.kind, c.params);
    out.writeln(field.model_name());
  }
}
```

